Z||000002||My7Fieros||09-16-2000||01:15 AM||||Oil sending unit needs to be replaced. Cheap and easy fix.. (around 20-30 bucks at Autozone) and about 5 minutes labor.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||09-16-2000||01:48 AM||||I'm trying to decide if I need an oil gauge or a sending unit too. My oil pressure in the 88 2.5L varies from 0-40. I noticed when I first bought the car that it was reading zero. I checked the oil which looked good and the guy had driven it about 25 miles to bring it to me. The temp was good. I knew it would have been dead if it really had no oil pressure.<P>I'm sure it is probably the sending unit and I have had to do so many other things to it that I haven't gotten around to that yet! But every now and then it sure does make me squirm when that thing is reading zero! I wish my gauge would jump every now and then! ||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FieroGTRacingInc||09-16-2000||01:51 AM||||Thank's Guy's...<BR>... oh  111,000 miles on a 87 GT...  <P>     steve||||reg||1
Z||000005||btoth||09-16-2000||12:57 PM||||where exaclty is the oil pressure sender located?  i've been meaning to replace mine as well.  TIA.||||reg||5
Z||000006||My7Fieros||09-16-2000||02:55 PM||||Btoth, if its a 6 cylinder, its located on the pass side of the block, on the top next to the thermostat housing. Its between the thermo housing and the rear window. Its got a quick connect plug that pulls off, then you unscrew the unit and replace. I took out the battery to alot myself more room. It only took me maybe 5 minutes to replace it.<BR>If its a 4 banger, I have no idea. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||btoth||09-16-2000||04:18 PM||||yes, it's a 6-cyl, sorry i didnt mention it.  thank you very much for the info!!<P>~ brian||||reg||7
